  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies remain fairly hazy and smoky due\nto the active wildfires across the western United States. Look for \nthe smoke to continue today, with minor impacts to visibility and \nair quality. Winds will be on the light side and no precipitation is \nexpected.\n\nThursday and Friday, the main story continues to be the warming \ntemperatures through the mid-week period. This is due to a fairly \nstrong upper level ridge that will undergo some strengthening as it \nretrogrades west in the coming day or two. As a result, high \ntemperatures will climb back into the triple digits in many \nlocations Thursday. Some record temperatures are sure to be broken \nduring the upcoming heatwave. Due to the intensity and duration of \nthe heat, there is an Extreme Heat Watch in effect for all of \nNorthern and Central Nevada from late Thursday morning until Sunday \nevening. High temperatures will climb into the upper 90s to low 100s \non Thursday and Friday. Overnight low temperatures will be in the \n50s and 60s for the period. Winds will generally remain light though \nsome occasional gusts to 20 mph are expected in the afternoon.\n\nSaturday and Sunday, the heat is expected to continue into the\nupcoming weekend, though model simulations are showing slight\nincreases in mid-level moisture. As the upper ridge shifts east \nto the four-corners region, look for this moisture to slowly make \nits way into Central Nevada. A low probability of isolated dry \nthunderstorms continues across portions of Central Nevada through \nthe weekend. Confidence remains low on the timing and coverage of \nstorms and may complicate current fire fighting efforts in Central \nNevada. This scenario will need to be watched for possible headline \nfire weather products. Afternoon high temperatures will continue to \nbe hot with readings in the upper 90s to low 100s. Overnight lows \nwill be in the 50s and 60s. One additional area of concern fire \nweather wise is across Northern Nevada, where breezy winds will \ncombine with low relative humidity values to bring elevated fire \nweather conditions. While wind speeds are slightly below RFW \ncriteria at this time, future model runs will need to be scrutinized \nfor Fire Weather product potential.\n\nMonday continues to look warm across the north with readings in\nthe upper 90s to low 100s remaining, though slightly cooler\ntemperatures in the low to mid 90s may bring some relief to the\nhot temperatures for Central Nevada.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence exists in \ncontinued smoky conditions affecting the area through the near term. \nHigh confidence also continues with hot temperatures returning \nThursday, prompting an Extreme Heat Watch for all locations through \nthe weekend. Moderate confidence exists for elevated fire weather \nconditions across Northern NV late this week. Low confidence in the \ntiming and placement for isolated dry thunderstorms continue for the \nweekend period across Central Nevada. Grid edits were not needed for \nNBM output at this time.",
